          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The white horse chiefly signifies victory or conquest. In Revelation 6:2 the rider is a conqueror, but whether that conqueror is Christ/the gospel, an antichrist figure, or generic imperial conquest remains disputed.\",\"book_summary\":\"The Revelation 6 white horse is the mount of a spiritually executing conqueror who subdues nations through a covenant-associated bow.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"White horse in Revelation 6:2 = victorious or conquering power, with the rider’s identity and moral character unsettled.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"White horse in Revelation 6:2 = the mount of a covenantal executing spirit who conquers nations.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book retains the consensus conquest identification but selects a particular spiritual and covenantal form of conquest. It addresses the Revelation 6 case rather than the broader range of white-horse imagery and does not admit the consensus’s generic imperial or deceptive-conqueror readings as its stated identification.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no contradiction with the settled consensus core that Revelation 6:2 depicts conquest. The book resolves an area that consensus leaves disputed by specifying the conqueror as an executing spirit and the bow as covenantal.\",\"rationale\":\"Reduced to minimum cores, both readings identify the Revelation 6 white horse with a conqueror. The book therefore does not replace the central consensus referent. It materially narrows and specifies that broad identification by assigning spiritual agency, national scope, and a covenant function to the bow. Those details go beyond the consensus while remaining compatible with its divine-agent or Christ/gospel alternatives.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Revelation 6:2 directly supports the horse, bow, crown, and conquest, but it does not by itself identify the rider as an “executing spirit,” call the bow covenantal, or explicitly mention conquering nations. Those distinctive refinements require support from the referenced chapter or additional texts.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
